/**
 * Case Study Data Layer
 *
 * Static data store for all case studies. Each case study follows a strict schema:
 * - slug: kebab-case, unique identifier matching /^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$/
 * - title: Display name
 * - category: Domain category
 * - problem: One-sentence business problem
 * - ownership: Ownership statement
 * - themes: Exactly 3 non-empty strings
 * - sections: Exactly 16 entries with types from {"text", "list", "diagram"}
 */

const caseStudies = [
  {
    slug: "checkcite",
    title: "Checkcite",
    category: "Enterprise SaaS",
    problem: "Digitising manual inspection and incident-resolution workflows for organisations that lacked visibility into ownership, deadlines, and escalation.",
    ownership: "System architecture, workflow design, backend APIs, frontend implementation, data modelling, and architectural evolution.",
    themes: ["Multi-organisation architecture", "RBAC", "Workflow automation"],
    sections: [
      { type: "text", heading: "Business Problem", content: "Before Checkcite, inspections were handled through a largely manual process. An inspector would visit a location, complete an inspection using spreadsheets or manually prepared checklists, and later return to the office to submit the results. Issues discovered during the inspection then had to be communicated, assigned, followed up, and escalated manually. This created several operational problems: inspection results were not immediately available to management, issue ownership was difficult to track, responsible handlers were not always notified promptly, deadlines required manual monitoring, overdue incidents could remain unresolved without escalation, management lacked a central view of operational issues, and customers could experience slower issue resolution. The central problem was not simply data collection. It was the absence of a dependable workflow connecting inspection, finding, issue, responsible handler, resolution deadline, and resolution or escalation." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Product Objective", content: "The objective was to create a platform that could manage the complete inspection lifecycle: inspection is assigned, inspector completes checklist, unsatisfactory response creates an issue, issue is assigned to a responsible handler, handler receives a notification, resolution deadline is tracked, unresolved issue becomes overdue, issue is escalated, and management receives visibility and control. The platform also needed to support multiple organisations while preserving organisational boundaries and role-based access." },
      { type: "text", heading: "My Role", content: "I worked across the technical delivery of the platform. My responsibilities included: translating operational requirements into software workflows, designing the initial application architecture, modelling organisations, users, inspections, issues, and permissions, building backend APIs, implementing the web application, designing role-based access behaviour, implementing inspection assignment workflows, implementing issue-generation and assignment workflows, connecting notification and escalation processes, and supporting the product's architectural evolution beyond the initial MVP. This was not simply an interface implementation. The work required converting an existing operational process into a structured and dependable software workflow." },
      { type: "list", heading: "Users & Responsibilities", items: ["Super Administrator: manages the broader platform and can operate across multiple organisations", "Organisation Administrator: manages users, roles, inspections, and operational activity within their organisation", "Supervisor: oversees inspections and incidents and can intervene when an issue becomes overdue or requires reassignment", "Inspector: receives inspection assignments, completes checklists, and records findings from the field", "Handler: responsible for resolving issues associated with particular checklist questions or operational areas"] },
      { type: "text", heading: "Architecture Decision", content: "The MVP had to be delivered within a limited timeframe. A microservices architecture would have introduced additional work around independent service deployment, inter-service communication, authentication between services, distributed tracing, event coordination, infrastructure management, data consistency across service boundaries, and monitoring multiple independently deployed components. Those costs were not justified for the initial product stage. We chose a modular monolith so that we could deliver the MVP quickly, keep deployment and operations manageable, maintain conceptual domain boundaries inside one application, simplify transactions and data access, reduce infrastructure complexity, and learn from real user behaviour before introducing distributed infrastructure. The decision was not that microservices were inherently unsuitable. It was that they were not proportionate to the product's maturity, delivery timeline, team capacity, or operational requirements." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "Domain Structure", description: "Application modules: Authentication, Organisations, Users and permissions, Inspections, Inspection categories, Facilities and locations, Questions and checklists, Issues, Reported incidents, Vehicles, Notifications, Dashboards, Audit activity, Escalation workflows. Key entities: Organisation, User, Inspection, Completed Inspection, Issue, Reported Incident, Category, Location, Facility, Vehicle, Question, Permission, Audit record. These boundaries allowed the system to retain a relatively simple deployment model without becoming an unstructured monolith." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Org Boundaries & Access Control", content: "The first version supported multiple organisations, while each individual user belonged to one organisation. Authorisation decisions combined user role, organisation membership, requested operation, and ownership or assignment context. Organisation selection in the interface was not treated as the security boundary. Backend queries and API operations also needed to restrict data according to the authenticated user, the user's organisation, the user's role, the requested resource, and the operation being attempted. The next version introduced support for users who could belong to more than one organisation, with login flow: user enters email, system identifies available organisations, user selects organisation, authentication completes, session is scoped to selected organisation. This provided greater flexibility but also introduced additional security requirements—every protected operation still needed to validate the selected organisation, the user's membership, the user's permissions within that organisation, and the requested resource's organisation." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Inspection/Issue Workflow", content: "Each inspection consisted of questions associated with operational responsibilities. When an inspector recorded an unsatisfactory answer: the platform created an issue, the issue was associated with the relevant inspection, the responsible handler was identified, a notification was initiated, a resolution deadline was assigned, the issue remained visible until it was resolved, overdue issues became eligible for escalation, and administrators could review or reassign responsibility. This converted each negative inspection result into a trackable unit of work rather than leaving it inside a spreadsheet, message thread, or email conversation." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Difficult Engineering Problem", content: "The most difficult part of the platform was not creating issues. It was ensuring that unresolved issues were detected and escalated at the correct time. The initial version operated under infrastructure and budget constraints. A dedicated worker system was not available, so scheduled escalation checks depended on an external scheduling service. That approach allowed the MVP to ship, but it introduced several operational weaknesses: scheduled jobs could run late, email delivery was not always dependable, failures were difficult to observe, retry behaviour was limited, missed executions were difficult to detect, escalation processing depended too closely on application availability, and administrators had limited ability to replay failed operations. This was an intentional MVP compromise, but it became less suitable as the escalation workflow became more operationally important." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Notification Ownership", content: "In the first implementation, part of the notification process was initiated from the frontend after issue-related API operations. That reduced initial backend complexity, but it made notification reliability dependent on the user's browser completing the workflow successfully. Potential failure scenarios included the browser closing after the issue was created, a network interruption before the notification request completed, duplicate frontend retries, inconsistent behaviour across different clients, and the business operation succeeding while the notification failed silently. The improved design moved notification responsibility to the backend. Once the backend successfully created an issue, it also became responsible for recording and dispatching the corresponding notification. This produced an important architectural lesson: a critical business side effect should be owned by the system that commits the underlying business operation, not by the client interface." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Version-Two Redesign", content: "The V2 redesign moved more responsibility for notifications, escalation, and asynchronous processing into the backend. The implemented improvements included a dedicated backend application, server-owned notification creation, scheduled background jobs, organisation-scoped sessions, multi-organisation user membership, retry handling, improved operational logging, and clearer failure visibility. The intended production-hardening direction—which should not be confused with deployed functionality—included a persistent job queue, durable escalation records, idempotent notification processing, delivery-attempt records, exponential retry policies, dead-letter handling for repeatedly failed jobs, alerts for missed escalation runs, administrative replay capability, and metrics for issue age and notification failures." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Scale & Adoption", content: "The platform supported multiple organisations, operational users, inspection workflows, and tracked incidents in active use. It reached approximately eight organisations or operational teams, more than 1,500 inspections, around 30 active users, and approximately 300 tracked incidents." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Trade-offs", content: "Modular monolith versus microservices: chose modular monolith for faster MVP delivery and lower operational complexity; cost was modules remained deployed together and independent scaling was more difficult. External scheduling versus dedicated workers: chose external scheduler for the MVP due to budget and infrastructure constraints; cost was reduced observability, weaker execution guarantees, and difficult failure recovery. Frontend-triggered notifications versus backend ownership: initially chose frontend-assisted notification flow for faster delivery; cost was notifications could become inconsistent when clients failed and successful issue creation did not guarantee notification delivery; improved by moving notification orchestration to the backend. Single-organisation membership versus multi-organisation membership: initially chose one organisation per user for simpler identity and authorisation model; cost was limited flexibility for users who operated across organisations; V2 direction introduced organisation selection and organisation-scoped sessions." },
      { type: "list", heading: "Lessons Learned", items: ["Architecture should match product maturity — the initial architecture needed to optimise for learning, delivery, and operational simplicity rather than hypothetical future scale. A technically sophisticated design is not automatically the correct design.", "Critical workflows require operational ownership — scheduled escalation and notification processing are not secondary features when the business depends on deadlines. They require clear ownership, monitoring, retry handling, failure visibility, and recovery mechanisms.", "The backend should own business-critical side effects — a client can request a business operation, but it should not be responsible for guaranteeing that essential downstream work occurs. Critical side effects should be initiated and tracked by the trusted backend system.", "Multi-organisation software requires more than an organisation selector — tenant isolation must be enforced across authentication, authorisation, session context, API operations, database queries, resource ownership, and audit records. Frontend state alone cannot enforce organisational boundaries.", "MVP decisions should be documented — a temporary compromise can quietly become permanent unless the team records why it was made, what risks it introduces, what signals should trigger redesign, and what the intended long-term architecture should become."] },
      { type: "text", heading: "Outcome", content: "Checkcite replaced a fragmented spreadsheet-and-manual-communication workflow with a centralised process for recording inspections, identifying unsatisfactory findings, creating issues, assigning responsibility, tracking deadlines, recording resolution status, escalating unresolved issues, and giving authorised users shared operational visibility. The platform made issue ownership explicit and provided a structured path from an inspection finding to assignment, resolution, or escalation. Its principal value was not merely digitising a checklist—it connected each finding to an accountable person, a deadline, and a visible resolution state." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Closing Statement", content: "Checkcite taught me that workflow software becomes valuable when it does more than store data. It must preserve ownership, deadlines, visibility, accountability, and resolution state throughout the complete business process. The project also reinforced an important engineering principle: architecture should be proportionate to the product's maturity, but business-critical workflows must still have clear operational guarantees." },
    ],
  },
  {
    slug: "distributed-wallet-engine",
    title: "Distributed Wallet Engine",
    category: "FinTech",
    problem: "Designing a wallet system that preserves balance consistency during concurrent transfers, prevents duplicate financial movements, records every debit and credit, and delivers transaction events reliably.",
    ownership: "Sole architect and engineer. System architecture, domain modelling, transaction processing, double-entry ledger design, database schema, idempotency handling, concurrency control, Kafka event processing, webhook delivery, audit logging, and operational dashboard development.",
    themes: ["Double-Entry Bookkeeping", "Idempotent Processing", "Event-Driven Architecture"],
    sections: [
      { type: "text", heading: "Business Problem", content: "A wallet transfer appears simple: deduct money from Wallet A, add money to Wallet B. In practice, several failure scenarios make this unsafe. Concurrent spending: two requests attempt to debit the same wallet simultaneously, and a naive implementation allows both to succeed even though only one can be funded. Duplicate requests: a client retries after a network timeout, and without durable idempotency the system moves money twice. Partial failures: the system debits the source wallet but fails before crediting the destination, or commits a transfer but fails before publishing its event. Auditability: every movement of funds should be reconstructable—which wallet was debited, which was credited, how much moved, who initiated it, when it occurred, and whether it was reversed. The project focused on four principal requirements: financial consistency, durable idempotency, complete auditability, and reliable event delivery." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Product Objective", content: "Design and implement a wallet-engine prototype demonstrating the following guarantees: a transfer either completes fully or has no financial effect, every transfer produces balanced debit and credit entries, concurrent requests cannot spend the same balance twice, repeated requests using the same idempotency key do not create duplicate transfers, monetary values are stored without floating-point precision errors, transaction events are not coupled to external webhook availability, failed webhook attempts are recorded with retry scheduling, reversals preserve the original financial history, wallet balances can be reconciled against ledger entries, and operators can inspect transaction and delivery state through a dashboard. The objective was to explore common financial-system patterns using TypeScript, PostgreSQL, Redis, Kafka, and asynchronous processing." },
      { type: "text", heading: "My Role", content: "I designed and built the project as a solo engineering exercise. My responsibilities included: defining the financial invariants, designing the application architecture, modelling wallets, transactions, ledger entries, webhooks, audit records, and outbox events, implementing transfer processing with PostgreSQL transactions and row-locking, implementing idempotency handling with Redis caching and PostgreSQL uniqueness constraints, designing the double-entry ledger with counterpart relationships and running balances, implementing transaction reversals with offsetting entries, building the transactional outbox flow, publishing events to Kafka, implementing webhook signing and delivery attempts with retry scheduling, recording audit events, building the Next.js operational dashboard, and documenting architectural trade-offs and failure scenarios." },
      { type: "list", heading: "Users & Responsibilities", items: ["Platform Engineers: integrate the wallet API into product flows using REST endpoints and idempotency keys, handle webhook callbacks for transaction status updates, verify webhook signatures using HMAC-SHA256", "Finance Operations: monitor transaction health through the dashboard, verify ledger integrity using reconciliation endpoints, investigate disputes using the audit trail", "End Users (indirect): perform deposits, withdrawals, and transfers through product interfaces that delegate to the engine", "System (Kafka consumers): process webhook dispatch and retry events from Kafka topics, deliver signed payloads to external endpoints with exponential backoff, record delivery attempts"] },
      { type: "text", heading: "Architecture Decision", content: "The engine is built with NestJS for its modular dependency injection—each domain concern (wallets, ledger, transfers, webhooks, audit) is a self-contained module with clear boundaries. PostgreSQL provides ACID transactions and row-level locking for financial correctness. Redis handles idempotency response caching and short-lived coordination locks where sub-millisecond latency matters. Apache Kafka provides durable event streaming for webhook delivery, decoupling the payment completion path from external notification. TypeORM maps the domain entities to PostgreSQL with native support for transaction managers and pessimistic locking. The architecture deliberately separates the synchronous financial write path from asynchronous notification delivery—a transfer does not wait for an external webhook endpoint before returning a successful response." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "Domain Structure", description: "Five PostgreSQL tables form the data model: wallets (one per user per currency, balance stored as bigint in smallest unit with unique constraint on user+currency), ledger_entries (two per transaction—one debit, one credit—linked via counterpart_entry_id, with running_balance for point-in-time reconstruction), transactions (lifecycle tracked via status_history JSONB array, linked to source and destination wallets, uniquely constrained by idempotency_key), outbox (domain events recorded in the same DB transaction as financial operations, published asynchronously by relay), webhooks (delivery tracking with attempt history in JSONB, retry scheduling, and HMAC-SHA256 signature), audit_logs (append-only, captures before/after state as JSONB for any entity change). The transfer flow: API request → Redis idempotency check → Redis coordination lock → PostgreSQL transaction with SELECT FOR UPDATE on wallets → create transaction + double-entry ledger entries + audit record + outbox event → commit → cache result in Redis → outbox relay publishes to Kafka → webhook consumer delivers to external endpoint." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Double-Entry Ledger", content: "The immutable ledger is the authoritative record of financial movements. Every wallet-to-wallet transfer creates exactly two corresponding entries—one debit and one credit—inside the same database transaction. They are linked to the same transaction, their respective wallets, and each other through a counterpart relationship. The transaction must satisfy: sum of debits equals sum of credits. Each entry records a running_balance that captures the wallet's balance at that exact moment, enabling point-in-time balance reconstruction without replaying the entire history. The wallet row also stores a materialised balance for efficient reads—every balance update occurs atomically with the corresponding ledger entries. LedgerService.createDoubleEntry() is the only place in the codebase that modifies wallet balances—this single controlled mutation path makes correctness auditable and bugs localisable. A reconciliation process can derive an expected balance from the ledger and compare it with the stored wallet balance." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Idempotent Transfer Processing", content: "Each transfer request includes a client-supplied idempotency key. The processing flow: check Redis for a cached response, if not found acquire a short-lived distributed lock for the key (Redis SET NX EX 30), query PostgreSQL for an existing transaction with that key, if found return its result, otherwise create the transaction inside the financial database transaction, commit ledger entries and balance updates and audit record and outbox event, cache the response in Redis with a 24-hour TTL, release the lock. The durable guarantee comes from PostgreSQL—the unique constraint on idempotency_key means only one database insert can succeed even if two application instances race. Redis is an optimisation rather than the final source of correctness, because cached values expire, Redis can become unavailable, and the application can crash after database commit but before caching the result." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Difficult Engineering Problem", content: "The hardest problem was preventing double-spending under concurrent load. PostgreSQL row-level locking serialises writes involving the same wallets. The transfer service acquires locks using SELECT FOR UPDATE—while one transaction holds a wallet lock, another transaction attempting to modify the same wallet must wait until the first commits or rolls back. Example: Wallet A contains ₦10,000, two transfers attempt to debit ₦8,000. Transfer 1 acquires the lock, validates the balance, deducts ₦8,000, and commits. Transfer 2 then acquires the lock, sees ₦2,000, and fails with insufficient funds. Pessimistic locking was selected because financial correctness was the principal priority, wallet conflicts were meaningful, the behaviour is simpler to reason about, and callers do not need to manage version-conflict retry loops. The cost is reduced concurrency on hot wallets—acceptable for this workload." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Transactional Outbox", content: "The transfer database transaction and Kafka publication cannot be treated as one atomic operation. Without an outbox, a crash between DB commit and Kafka publish loses the event. Publishing before commit creates the opposite risk—consumers receive events for rolled-back transactions. The outbox addresses this by recording the event inside the same PostgreSQL transaction as the financial operation. After commit, a separate relay process queries unpublished outbox records, publishes each to Kafka, and records the result. The pattern provides at-least-once publication rather than exactly-once delivery—a crash after Kafka accepts the message but before marking the outbox record as published may cause duplicate publication. Consumers must therefore be idempotent. This decouples financial completion from Kafka availability: the transfer succeeds even if Kafka is temporarily unreachable." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Webhook Delivery Pipeline", content: "When a transfer completes, external systems may need transaction updates. The webhook flow: outbox relay publishes event to Kafka, webhook consumer receives the message, creates or retrieves a webhook record, signs the payload using HMAC-SHA256 (included in X-Webhook-Signature header), sends an HTTP POST to the registered endpoint, and records the attempt result including response code, error, and duration. On failure, the webhook is scheduled for retry with exponential backoff (5 seconds, 30 seconds, 5 minutes). After the configured retry limit, the webhook is marked as permanently failed. Every delivery attempt is recorded in a JSONB array for debugging. Receiving systems verify authenticity by computing an HMAC from the raw body using the shared secret and comparing it with the signature header using a timing-safe operation." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Audit & Operational Dashboard", content: "Every significant action writes an immutable record to the audit_logs table: wallet created, frozen, unfrozen; transaction created, completed, failed, reversed; ledger entries created; webhooks dispatched, delivered, failed. Each entry captures the before and after state as JSONB, the initiating user, and a precise timestamp. The table is append-only—nothing is ever updated or deleted. The Next.js operational dashboard provides views into wallets, balances, transactions, transaction status, ledger entries with running balances, webhook attempts and delivery status, audit records, and infrastructure health checks for PostgreSQL, Redis, and Kafka connectivity. The ledger verification endpoint sums all entries for a transaction and confirms the net is zero, enabling programmatic integrity checks." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Trade-offs", content: "Pessimistic locking over optimistic: chose SELECT FOR UPDATE because wallet conflicts are meaningful and the behaviour is simpler to reason about; cost is reduced concurrency on hot wallets and deadlock risk. Materialised balance plus ledger: stored a wallet balance for fast reads while retaining the immutable ledger as source of truth; cost is two representations that must remain consistent through atomic updates and reconciliation. PostgreSQL constraint plus Redis cache: PostgreSQL provides durable uniqueness while Redis accelerates repeated requests; cost is additional infrastructure and cache-expiry edge cases. Transactional outbox over direct Kafka: avoided losing committed events or publishing events for rolled-back transactions; cost is an additional table, relay process, and duplicate-publication possibility requiring idempotent consumers. Integer minor units over floating-point: stored all amounts as bigint in smallest currency unit eliminating precision bugs entirely; cost is conversion logic at API boundaries. No cross-currency transfers: deliberately rejected because FX conversion introduces exchange rates, spreads, settlement, and regulatory concerns that belong in a separate domain." },
      { type: "list", heading: "Lessons Learned", items: ["Financial correctness must be designed as a set of invariants enforced through database transactions, unique constraints, ledger rules, locking strategies, and controlled mutation paths—not through developers remembering every edge case", "Idempotency is a durable business guarantee that must survive cache expiry, service restarts, network retries, concurrent requests, and multiple application instances—a temporary cache entry alone is not sufficient protection against duplicate financial operations", "The ledger and balance serve different purposes: the ledger preserves financial history, the wallet balance provides efficient access to current state—maintaining both requires atomic updates and reconciliation", "At-least-once delivery requires idempotent consumers: the transactional outbox reduces the risk of losing events but duplicate publication remains possible, so downstream consumers must process repeated events safely", "External availability should not determine financial correctness: a slow or unavailable webhook endpoint should not cause a completed transfer to roll back—financial completion and external notification belong to separate reliability boundaries", "Reversals should preserve history: financial records should be corrected through new offsetting entries rather than deleting or mutating historical movements"] },
      { type: "text", heading: "Outcome", content: "The Distributed Wallet Engine is an independent engineering prototype demonstrating how financial-system concerns combine within one coherent transaction architecture. The project covers: wallet-to-wallet transfers with double-entry ledger records, transactionally maintained wallet balances with PostgreSQL row-level locking, durable database idempotency constraints with Redis caching and coordination, transactional outbox processing with Kafka-based asynchronous events, signed webhook delivery with retry scheduling and attempt tracking, immutable audit records, transaction reversals with offsetting entries, and operational inspection through a Next.js dashboard. The project was not deployed as a production payment platform and did not process real customer funds. Its value lies in demonstrating financial invariants, database transaction design, concurrency control, failure analysis, event-delivery semantics, and engineering trade-offs within a single coherent system." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Closing Statement", content: "The Distributed Wallet Engine taught me that financial-system correctness comes from explicitly constraining what the system is allowed to do. A controlled ledger mutation path reduces the number of places where balances can change. A database uniqueness constraint prevents one transaction intent from producing multiple transfers. Row-level locks serialise conflicting wallet updates. An outbox connects committed financial state to asynchronous event publication. Offsetting ledger entries preserve history during reversals. Each constraint eliminates a class of failure rather than relying on reactive fixes after inconsistencies appear. Reliable financial systems are built by making invalid states difficult—or impossible—to represent and commit." },
    ],
  },
  {
    slug: "ppp-partner-portal",
    title: "PPP Partner Portal",
    category: "GovTech",
    problem: "OYNTCDA needed a structured digital system for managing Public-Private Partnership projects across opportunity publishing, Expressions of Interest, payments, proposal review, contracts, signatures, milestones, and project close-out.",
    ownership: "Full-stack architect and sole engineer. Requirements analysis, domain modelling, system architecture, backend development, frontend development, authentication, role-based access, payment integration, contract workflows, PDF generation, notifications, asynchronous processing, and deployment.",
    themes: ["Workflow Orchestration", "Domain Modelling", "Contract Lifecycle Management"],
    sections: [
      { type: "text", heading: "Business Problem", content: "Public-Private Partnership projects require coordinated interaction between government administrators and private companies. Before the platform, important parts of this process depended on manual coordination: government administrators announcing opportunities, private companies submitting Expressions of Interest through email or physical documents, administrators recording and evaluating submissions manually, partners making submission payments separately, shortlisted organisations preparing proposals, contract terms being exchanged through documents, parties arranging physical signing, and milestone progress being tracked through meetings, calls, and spreadsheets. This created several risks: submissions could be difficult to trace, payment records could become separated from the relevant application, administrators lacked one source of truth for project status, partners could not easily see the current stage of their submissions, contract versions could become inconsistent, responsibilities and deadlines were difficult to monitor, and audit history was fragmented across different communication channels. The central problem was not merely digitising a form—it was orchestrating a long-running, multi-party workflow in which each stage had different participants, permissions, preconditions, documents, status transitions, notifications, payment requirements, and audit requirements." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Product Objective", content: "Build a platform that models the PPP lifecycle as a series of explicit workflows: government creates project, project is published, partner discovers opportunity, partner submits Expression of Interest, submission fee is initiated and verified, EOI is reviewed, partner is shortlisted or rejected, shortlisted partner submits proposal, proposal is evaluated, contract terms are prepared, partner signs, administrator signs, contract becomes active, milestones are tracked, project is completed or closed out. The system needed to ensure that users could not bypass required stages: an unpaid EOI should not be treated as paid, a partner should not submit a proposal unless shortlisted, a contract should not become active before required parties sign, a contract should not be closed out while required milestones remain incomplete, and a partner should not access another partner's submissions or contracts." },
      { type: "text", heading: "My Role", content: "I served as the sole engineer responsible for the technical delivery of the platform. My work included: analysing the operational PPP workflow, translating business requirements into domain models, designing the overall system architecture, building the Django REST Framework backend, building the Next.js frontend, designing PostgreSQL schemas, implementing JWT authentication with OTP 2FA, implementing administrator and partner roles, building project and EOI workflows, integrating Paga for EOI submission payments, building proposal-management workflows, designing contract state transitions, implementing partner and administrator signing flows, generating contract PDFs with ReportLab, implementing milestone management, building notifications and background tasks with Celery, handling document and signature uploads via Cloudinary, and supporting deployment. The principal challenge was ensuring that the software represented the business process correctly rather than becoming a generic document-management application." },
      { type: "list", heading: "Users & Responsibilities", items: ["Administrator (OYNTCDA Staff): creates and publishes PPP projects, defines sector classifications and eligibility criteria, reviews Expressions of Interest, reviews payment status, shortlists or rejects partners, reviews proposals, prepares contract terms with milestones and KPIs, signs contracts after the partner, monitors active contracts, updates milestone progress, completes contract close-out, manages administrative users through invitations", "Partner (Private Companies): creates a business profile with required documentation, views published projects, submits Expressions of Interest, pays submission fees through Paga, views payment status, receives shortlist decisions, submits proposals with implementation plans, reviews contract terms, signs contracts when invited, tracks active contracts and milestone progress, withdraws where the workflow permits", "System (Automated): verifies payment callbacks and updates EOI status, generates payment references, sends email notifications via Celery tasks, generates contract PDFs from structured data, records signature timestamps, runs scheduled milestone checks via Celery Beat, identifies and notifies overdue milestones to both partner and administrator"] },
      { type: "text", heading: "Architecture Decision", content: "The platform was built as a structured monolithic application because one engineer owned the full system, the workflows shared a strongly related relational data model, many state transitions required transactional consistency, independent microservice deployments would have increased operational overhead, the product needed to evolve quickly while requirements were changing, and a monolith simplified authentication, data access, testing, and deployment. The backend separated responsibilities into service functions (business operations), selector functions (data retrieval filtered by authenticated user and role), API views (thin wrappers that validate input and call services), permissions, domain models, background tasks, payment handlers, PDF generation, and notification processing. The frontend uses Next.js with App Router, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, and ShadCN UI. Celery with Redis handles async work: sending notification emails, processing payment webhook confirmations, and running scheduled milestone overdue checks." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "Domain Structure", description: "Core domain entities: Project (with lifecycle states: draft, published, contracted, monitoring, closed_out, archived), Sector (categorises projects by government ministry), EOI (Expression of Interest linked to Project and Partner, with states: draft, submitted, under_review, approved, rejected, withdrawn, and payment status: unpaid, processing, paid), Payment (Paga transaction record with reference, amount in NGN, status, gateway response, webhook verification), Proposal (linked to EOI with implementation plan, states: draft, submitted, under_review, approved, rejected, withdrawn), Contract (linked to Proposal, contains milestones, KPIs, SLAs, scope, financial terms, states: draft, under_review, pending_signature, monitoring, active, terminated, completed, expired, cancelled, withdrawn), Milestone (linked to Contract, with due date, completion status, completion timestamp), User (Admin or Partner role, with email-based OTP 2FA), Partner (business profile with registration details), Notification (multi-channel: in-app and email, with user preferences and delivery tracking)." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Role-Based Data Isolation", content: "Partners and administrators operate within different data-access contexts. A partner should only access: published projects, their own business profile, their own Expressions of Interest, their own payment attempts, their own proposals, contracts to which they are a party, milestones associated with their contracts, and notifications intended for them. An administrator may access projects in all lifecycle states, partner submissions, payment records, proposals, contracts, milestones, and administrative reporting. The frontend is not the security boundary—backend APIs enforce access using authenticated identity, user role, resource ownership, partner relationship, requested operation, and object status. Hiding another partner's proposal in the user interface is not sufficient; the API must reject any request attempting to retrieve or modify that resource. Access enforcement is implemented through selectors that filter querysets by the authenticated user, and custom permission classes that validate group membership before any data access." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Workflow and State Transitions", content: "The platform contains several connected state machines. Each transition validates preconditions: an EOI should not be treated as paid until payment is verified via webhook, a proposal should not be accepted unless it has been submitted, a contract should not be activated before required signatures exist, a contract should not be closed out before milestone requirements are met, and a terminated or completed contract should not accept ordinary milestone updates. Each transition is implemented as a service function that retrieves relevant records, validates user permissions, validates current status, validates related domain preconditions, applies changes transactionally, records audit information, dispatches required side effects, and returns the resulting state. Invalid transitions return clear domain errors rather than silently changing state." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Difficult Engineering Problem", content: "The most difficult engineering problem was coordinating multiple dependent workflows without allowing invalid combinations of state. The platform contains relationships: Project → EOI → Payment → Proposal → Contract → Signatures → Milestones. A failure in one stage affects what is permitted in later stages. A payment callback may arrive twice. A payment may complete after a user has left the page. A proposal may be submitted while the EOI status is changing. One party may sign while the contract is being edited. A milestone may be updated after the contract has terminated. A close-out request may occur while milestones remain incomplete. The solution was placing transition logic in explicit service functions where each service validates preconditions, applies changes transactionally, and dispatches side effects—making workflow rules visible, testable, and reusable rather than scattered across views and serializers." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Payment Integration", content: "Expressions of Interest require payment of a submission fee through Paga, a Nigerian payment gateway. The flow: partner initiates payment, backend creates a Payment record and generates Paga widget parameters, partner completes payment through Paga, Paga calls the webhook endpoint, backend validates the callback using SHA-512 hash verification, payment status is updated, and EOI payment status is updated. The handler must ensure idempotent processing: the payment reference identifies one record, a paid payment is not credited twice, repeated callbacks do not create duplicate records, an EOI already marked as paid does not transition repeatedly, and conflicting gateway responses are logged. The database enforces one active payment per EOI through a unique constraint on the combination of EOI and active payment status." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Contract Signing Workflow", content: "The implemented signing sequence requires the partner to sign first and the administrator to sign afterwards—the government should not countersign an agreement the partner has not accepted. The contract records partner_signature_url, partner_signed_at, admin_signature_url, and admin_signed_at. The partner may provide signatory information including full name, designation, and email. The system supports token-based invitation links with expiry for additional signatories. The platform records electronic-signing events and signature evidence including timestamps and authenticated identity, but should not be described as eIDAS-compliant, legally certified, or providing guaranteed non-repudiation without formal legal and security assessment. A more accurate description: the platform supports traceable electronic contract signing through authenticated workflows, signature records, timestamps, and audit information. The platform generates contract documents from structured data using ReportLab, including contract title, reference number, party information, dates, scope, commercial terms, responsibilities, KPIs, SLAs, milestones, and signature blocks. The generator handles optional fields explicitly—missing dates, absent signatures, and long text must not crash the generation process. Contract close-out requires that all required milestones are complete before the transition is permitted." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Notification & Async Processing", content: "The platform sends notifications at workflow transitions: EOI submitted, payment confirmed, payment failed, EOI shortlisted, EOI rejected, proposal invited, proposal submitted, contract created, contract ready for signing, signature completed, contract activated, milestone overdue. Notifications are dispatched asynchronously via Celery tasks to avoid blocking API responses. Celery Beat runs scheduled tasks to detect overdue milestones and dispatch notifications to both the responsible partner and supervising administrator. However, Celery configuration alone does not constitute reliable delivery—reliable notification processing also requires worker availability, broker availability, retry policies, SMTP provider availability, monitoring, failure visibility, and operational recovery procedures. These production-hardening concerns are acknowledged as ongoing improvement areas." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Trade-offs", content: "Structured monolith versus microservices: one engineer maintained the system, workflows shared relational data, distributed transactions were unnecessary, deployment simplicity mattered, and requirements were still evolving; cost is modules share deployment and independent scaling is limited. Service-layer business logic versus logic in views: state transitions can be tested independently and reused outside HTTP requests; cost is additional abstraction and need for consistent conventions. Local PDF generation versus commercial document service: greater control over content and lower cost; cost is more layout complexity, font handling, and testing responsibility. Asynchronous notifications versus synchronous delivery: email-provider latency does not block API responses and failed delivery can be retried; cost is additional infrastructure and need for worker monitoring. Partner-first sequential signing: the government should not countersign an agreement the partner has not accepted; cost is administrator signing must wait and expired invitations can delay activation. Paga as required payment provider: client requirement with Nigerian Naira support; cost is provider-specific callback handling, hash verification requirements, and dependency on provider availability." },
      { type: "list", heading: "Lessons Learned", items: ["Complex workflows need explicit state transitions—PPP processes involve more than CRUD operations, and each stage has preconditions, permissions, side effects, and audit requirements that must be made visible in service functions rather than scattered across views", "Payment callbacks must be idempotent—a payment provider may send the same callback multiple times, and correctness must come from unique references, state checks, and transactional updates rather than assuming one callback per payment", "Signing is a workflow, not a boolean—the system must understand who signed, which party they represent, when they signed, whether they were authorised, and whether earlier required signatures exist", "Backend permissions are the real security boundary—hiding information in the frontend does not prevent unauthorised API access, and resource access must be enforced in backend queries and services", "Background automation requires monitoring—a scheduled task that fails silently creates false confidence, and milestone checks and notifications require observable execution, retries, and recovery procedures", "Generated documents must handle incomplete data—contract PDFs must safely handle optional fields, missing dates, absent signatures, and long text without crashing"] },
      { type: "text", heading: "Outcome", content: "The PPP Partner Portal created a structured digital workflow for managing PPP opportunities, partner participation, Expressions of Interest, submission payments, proposal review, contract preparation, electronic-signing steps, contract milestones, and project close-out. The platform centralised information that would otherwise be distributed across emails, spreadsheets, documents, gateway records, and manual follow-up. Its main engineering value was the explicit modelling of lifecycle states, transition preconditions, user permissions, payment status, contract status, signing order, milestone dependencies, and party responsibilities—making the rules that connect each stage visible and enforceable rather than implicit in manual processes." },
      { type: "text", heading: "Closing Statement", content: "The PPP Partner Portal demonstrated that complex government workflows can be modelled as explicit software state machines. The important engineering challenge was not creating pages for projects, payments, proposals, and contracts independently—it was preserving the rules that connect them. A payment must belong to the correct Expression of Interest. A proposal must originate from an eligible partner. A contract must originate from an approved process. The correct party must sign at the correct stage. A contract cannot close while required obligations remain incomplete. Reliable workflow software does not merely store the current status—it protects the rules that determine how the system is allowed to move from one state to another." },
    ],
  },
];

/**
 * Retrieve a single case study by slug.
 * @param {string} slug - The kebab-case slug identifier
 * @returns {object|null} The case study object or null if not found
 */
export function getCaseStudy(slug) {
  return caseStudies.find((cs) => cs.slug === slug) || null;
}

/**
 * Get all case study slugs.
 * @returns {string[]} Array of unique slug strings
 */
export function getAllCaseStudySlugs() {
  return caseStudies.map((cs) => cs.slug);
}

/**
 * Get the 3 featured case studies in display order.
 * @returns {object[]} Array of exactly 3 case study objects
 */
export function getFeaturedCaseStudies() {
  const featured = ["checkcite", "distributed-wallet-engine", "ppp-partner-portal"];
  return featured.map((slug) => getCaseStudy(slug)).filter(Boolean);
}
